# Plan commit workflow (Cursor-specific)

Personal flow optimization — not project documentation. Do not add this workflow to `AGENTS.md` or feature docs.

## When creating or updating a plan

### 1. Baseline snapshot

In each repo that may be affected, run read-only git commands and record results in a **Commit strategy** section:

| Repo path | Typical changes |
| --------- | --------------- |
| `/workspaces/silentorb-workbench/.mnt/tome/` | Packages, tests, `docs/features/` |
| `/workspaces/silentorb-workbench/.mnt/marloth-story/` | `content/`, Marloth docs |
| Workbench root | Devcontainer, scripts, root `AGENTS.md` |

Commands per repo: `git status --short`, `git diff`, `git diff --staged` (if staged), `git log -3 --oneline`.

### 2. Classify dirty files

Label each changed path:

- **Pre-existing** — dirty before this plan
- **Plan-scoped** — will change during implementation
- **Unrelated** — out of scope; exclude from plan commits

**Default: greedy include.** In each affected repo, include **all pre-existing dirty files** in that repo’s planned commit unless they are clearly unrelated to the work or look like secrets. Prefer bundling prior local residue with the plan commit over leaving it uncommitted. Ask only when something is clearly unrelated or sensitive — do not default to “exclude.”

### 3. Per-repo commit plan

Default: **one commit per affected repo** (not one squashed commit across repos).

For each affected repo specify: files to include, files to exclude, draft commit message (1–2 sentences, why not what; match recent `git log` style).

### 4. Final todo (always last)

Add frontmatter todo `commit-await-approval` as the **last** item:

> Commit (await user approval) — After implementation and verification, re-run git status/diff per repo, reconcile with this strategy, commit only when the user explicitly requests it.

Keep `pending` during planning and implementation unless the user asks to commit.

### 5. Commit strategy section template

```markdown
## Commit strategy

### Baseline (plan created)
- /workspaces/silentorb-workbench/.mnt/tome: clean | dirty — list pre-existing files
- /workspaces/silentorb-workbench/.mnt/marloth-story: …
- workbench: …

### Intended commits
#### /workspaces/silentorb-workbench/.mnt/tome (tome repo)
- Include: …
- Exclude: …
- Draft message: "…"

### User trigger
Say **commit** (or **commit per the plan**) after review. Say **preview bump** to see what a release would bump (advisory). Say **commit and preview bump** to commit then print that preview. Say **bump** alone when ready to release (work already committed). Say **commit and bump** when a tome release tag is wanted in one go.
```

## After plan implementation (before awaiting commit)

When implementation and verification finish — and **before** stopping on `commit-await-approval` — print a short **post-implementation summary** that includes:

1. **Commit scope refresh** — re-run `git status --short` per affected repo; note drift from the plan’s include/exclude list (do not commit yet).

**Do not** print a bump preview automatically after implementation (or after plain **commit**). Preview only when the user says **preview bump** or **commit and preview bump**.

Plans do not need a full version matrix in the Commit strategy section — run **preview bump** when a release classification is wanted.

## Commit execution

Only when the user explicitly says "commit" / "go ahead and commit" / "commit per the plan":

1. Re-snapshot git state in each affected repo.
2. Compare to the plan; if scope differs materially, pause and confirm.
3. Follow the user's `committing-changes-with-git` rule: status + diff + log in parallel, stage only planned files, HEREDOC message, verify after.
4. Repeat per affected repo. Never force-push or amend unless user rules allow.

**Do not** run `bump-version`, refresh lockfiles for versioning, bump the repo-root release version, or create git tags on plain **commit**.
**Do not** print a bump preview after plain **commit**.

Do **not** auto-commit when implementation finishes.

## Preview bump

When the user says **preview bump** (alone, or after other work):

1. Find the latest local tome tag matching `v*` (e.g. `git describe --tags --match 'v*' --abbrev=0` in `.mnt/tome`). If none, treat the full history / settled range as in-scope.
2. Analyze packages touched **since that tag…HEAD** in `.mnt/tome` and `.mnt/imp-ts` (include uncommitted changes only if they are part of the settled release range the user is previewing — prefer committed history; note dirty trees if present).
3. Print the advisory preview. Do **not** run bump scripts, refresh lockfiles for versioning, create tags, or commit.

Preview must list:

| Item | What to print |
| ---- | ------------- |
| Workspace packages | Each touched package: current version → proposed `minor` or `patch` next version, one-line why |
| Cascades | Dependents that would move because of a `minor` bump / `workspace:` range rewrite |
| Root release package | e.g. `tome` root: current → proposed next version + level |
| Resulting git tag | e.g. `v0.3.0` matching the proposed root version |
| Ordinary commit note | Remind that plain **commit** does **not** bump versions or create tags; only **bump** / **commit and bump** do |

Classify `minor` vs `patch` using that repo’s versioning rules (tome: 0.x — `MINOR` = API epoch / new functionality or breaking; `PATCH` = backwards-compatible fixes). Treat the preview as advisory; **re-classify at bump time** if the diff changed.

## Commit and preview bump

When the user says **commit and preview bump**:

1. Run **commit** execution for all planned repos (work commits as-is; no version bumps).
2. Then run **preview bump** (advisory only — no bump scripts or tags).

## Bump

When the user says **bump** (tome release; today the only taggable repo):

1. Require **clean** working trees in every repo that will receive a version commit (at least `.mnt/tome`; also `.mnt/imp-ts` when imp packages will bump). If dirty, refuse and tell the user to **commit** or **commit and bump** first.
2. Find the latest local tome tag matching `v*` (e.g. `git describe --tags --match 'v*' --abbrev=0` in `.mnt/tome`). If none, treat the full history / settled range as in-scope.
3. Classify packages touched **since that tag…HEAD** in `.mnt/tome` and `.mnt/imp-ts` (`minor` vs `patch` per 0.x rules). Do **not** require a prior **preview bump**; if one was printed earlier, still re-classify from current HEAD.
4. Run `bash scripts/bump-version.sh <package> <level>` for each touched **workspace** package (cascades/ranges as the script already does), then bump root `tome` (`minor` if any workspace minor / release-worthy API epoch; else `patch`). Refresh lockfiles as in **Version bumps** below.
5. Commit **version + lockfile only** changes — one commit per affected repo — with messages matching recent `git log` style (why-focused).
6. Create a **local** annotated tag: `bash scripts/git-tag-version.sh tome` (`v<version>` matching root `package.json`).
7. **Do not push** the commit(s) or the tag. Report the tag name so the user can push when ready (e.g. for GHCR semver).

Supported tag repos today: `tome` → `.mnt/tome`.

## Commit and bump

When the user says **commit and bump**:

1. Run **commit** execution for all planned repos (work commits as-is; no version bumps).
2. Then run **bump** (dedicated version commits + local `v*` tag).

This is always a **two-step** history: work commit(s) first, then bump commit(s) + tag — never fold the root version bump into the feature commit.

**Do not** print a bump preview as part of **commit and bump** unless the user also asked for preview.

## Version bumps (reconcile at bump time)

Plans may include draft bump levels and lockfile notes — treat these as **starting points**, not contracts. The same tension applies as commit scope:

- Complex plans cannot list every file change.
- The user may request additional changes after implementation and **before** committing or bumping — expected, not plan failure.
- **At bump time**, re-snapshot commits since the last `v*` tag (or the settled range), re-classify bump levels (`minor` vs `patch`) for each touched package, run `bash scripts/bump-version.sh` from the workbench root as needed, refresh lockfiles (`bun install` in each repo root whose lockfile resolves affected dependents), and include version + lockfile changes in the **bump** commit(s).
- Version bumps happen **after** the change set is settled and **committed**, not when the plan is written and not during plain **commit**.
- Root release versions (e.g. `tome`) bump only on **bump** / **commit and bump**, never on ordinary commits.
- A **preview bump** is advisory; always re-run the classification when executing bump.

Lockfile refresh rule: any imp-ts package change → refresh both `.mnt/imp-ts` and `.mnt/tome` lockfiles; tome-only changes → `.mnt/tome` only.
